/* Bassiet book, Depth 14: The Ossuary Wall (bone, joint, muscle and skin). Original text. */
window.BOOK=window.BOOK||{};
BOOK['c14-bone']={depth:14,title:'The Lattice',
epi:'A lattice of bone, rebuilt every day by two crews: one that digs and one that lays.',
c:['osteoporosis','bone-disease','calcium-pth','collagen','vitamins','genetic-disorders'],
S:[
{h:'Building and remodelling',p:[
'**Osteoblasts** (from mesenchymal stem cells, driven by RUNX2 and Wnt signalling) lay down osteoid, mostly type I collagen, and mineralise it with hydroxyapatite; their alkaline phosphatase marks bone formation. They also express **RANKL**, which binds **RANK** on **osteoclast** precursors (monocyte-macrophage lineage, with M-CSF) and drives their fusion into multinucleated resorbing cells. **Osteoprotegerin** from osteoblasts is a decoy receptor that mops up RANKL. Osteoclasts seal onto bone and dissolve it with H⁺ (from carbonic anhydrase II) and cathepsin K in Howship lacunae. **Osteocytes**, osteoblasts buried in the matrix, sense strain and release **sclerostin**, which brakes formation; loading the skeleton lowers sclerostin, which is why weight-bearing builds bone. Intermittent PTH and oestrogen favour formation; oestrogen loss and continuous PTH favour resorption.',
'**Endochondral ossification** (long bones, vertebrae, pelvis) replaces a cartilage model and lengthens bone at the growth plate, where chondrocytes proliferate, swell, die and are replaced by bone; growth hormone acts there through IGF-1, and oestrogen, in both sexes, finally fuses the plate. **Membranous ossification** (skull vault, face, clavicle) lays bone directly in mesenchyme. **Achondroplasia** (autosomal dominant activating FGFR3 mutation, mostly new mutations linked to older fathers) slows growth-plate chondrocytes: short limbs, a normal trunk, a large head, normal intelligence.'],
table:{cols:['Disorder','Ca²⁺','PO₄³⁻','ALP','PTH'],rows:[
['Osteoporosis','normal','normal','normal','normal'],
['Osteomalacia / rickets (vitamin D lack)','↓ or normal','↓','↑','↑'],
['Paget disease','normal','normal','↑↑','normal'],
['Primary hyperparathyroidism','↑','↓','↑ or normal','↑'],
['Renal osteodystrophy (CKD)','↓','↑','↑','↑↑'],
['Hypoparathyroidism','↓','↑','normal','↓'],
['Vitamin D excess, granulomas','↑','↑','normal','↓'],
['Osteopetrosis','normal or ↓','normal','normal','normal or ↑']]},
s:[['phys','Calcium and phosphate are kept in balance by PTH, active vitamin D (calcitriol) and FGF23; the full ledger, with the parathyroid matrix, is kept in the Bone Clerks.']]},
{h:'Bone diseases',p:[
'**Osteoporosis**: low bone mass and disordered microarchitecture with normal mineralisation, so the labs are normal. Primary: postmenopausal oestrogen loss (trabecular bone first) and age. Secondary: glucocorticoids, smoking, alcohol, low body weight, hyperthyroidism, hypogonadism, heparin, proton pump inhibitors, aromatase inhibitors, immobility. Fractures: vertebral crush (kyphosis, height loss, often painless), hip, and distal radius (Colles) after a fall from standing height. DEXA T-score ≤ −2.5 (or any fragility fracture).',
'**Osteomalacia** (adults) and **rickets** (children): osteoid fails to mineralise. Causes: vitamin D deficiency (little sun, dark skin at high latitude, malabsorption, liver or kidney disease, enzyme-inducing anticonvulsants), phosphate wasting (X-linked hypophosphataemic rickets: FGF23 excess, low phosphate with normal calcium) and rare vitamin D receptor defects. Children: bowed legs, swollen costochondral junctions (rachitic rosary), soft skull (craniotabes), widened wrists, delayed fontanelle closure. Adults: diffuse bone pain, proximal myopathy, Looser zones (pseudofractures).',
'**Paget disease of bone**: older adults; a burst of osteoclast resorption, then chaotic osteoblast repair, giving thick but weak "mosaic" bone with cement lines. Bone pain, enlarging skull and hat size, deafness (ossicles and nerve compression), bowed tibia, high-output heart failure (arteriovenous shunting in vascular bone), osteosarcoma risk; very high ALP with normal calcium; bisphosphonates. **Osteopetrosis**: osteoclasts fail (carbonic anhydrase II defect among others): dense but brittle bone, marrow failure with extramedullary haematopoiesis, cranial nerve compression; stem cell transplant (osteoclasts come from marrow). **Osteogenesis imperfecta**: defective type I collagen (COL1A1/2): fractures from minor trauma (can mimic abuse), blue sclerae, hearing loss, dental imperfections. **Fibrous dysplasia**: bone replaced by fibrous tissue and woven bone ("ground glass"); with café-au-lait spots and endocrine overactivity it is McCune-Albright syndrome (mosaic activating GNAS mutation).'],
s:[['path','Avascular necrosis: steroids, alcohol, sickle cell disease, trauma (femoral neck, scaphoid), decompression sickness, SLE, Gaucher disease; femoral head commonest. Osteomyelitis: S. aureus most often; Salmonella is characteristic in sickle cell disease (S. aureus is still common); Pseudomonas after a nail through a trainer; Pasteurella after cat or dog bites; S. epidermidis on prosthetic material; Pott disease (TB) in the thoracic spine. Children seed the vascular metaphysis; adults who inject drugs seed the vertebrae. MRI is the most sensitive test; raised ESR and CRP.'],['pharm','Bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, zoledronic acid) are pyrophosphate analogues that bind hydroxyapatite and trigger osteoclast apoptosis when ingested; take tablets upright with a full glass of water, 30 minutes before food, to avoid oesophagitis and allow absorption. Rare: osteonecrosis of the jaw, atypical femoral fractures. Denosumab (anti-RANKL antibody): stopping it abruptly causes rebound bone loss and vertebral fractures. Teriparatide and abaloparatide (intermittent PTH and PTHrP analogues) build bone; avoid in Paget disease or after skeletal radiation. Romosozumab (anti-sclerostin) builds bone but may raise cardiovascular risk. Raloxifene (SERM) protects vertebrae. Calcium and vitamin D replete the base.']]},
{h:'Bone tumours',p:[
'Metastases are by far the commonest bone tumours (breast, lung, prostate, kidney, thyroid); prostate metastases are **osteoblastic** (sclerotic), most others lytic, and breast can be either. **Multiple myeloma** (a marrow cancer) is the commonest primary malignancy arising within bone in older adults, with punched-out lytic lesions; osteosarcoma is the commonest primary bone sarcoma. Location along the bone helps: epiphysis (giant cell tumour, chondroblastoma), metaphysis (osteosarcoma, osteochondroma), diaphysis (Ewing sarcoma).'],
table:{cols:['Tumour','Age and site','Clues'],rows:[
['Osteochondroma','young men, metaphysis','commonest benign bone tumour; cartilage-capped outgrowth continuous with the marrow; EXT1/2; rarely becomes chondrosarcoma'],
['Enchondroma','adults, small bones of hands and feet','benign cartilage in the medulla; many in Ollier disease, with haemangiomas in Maffucci syndrome'],
['Giant cell tumour','20–40, epiphysis around the knee','"soap bubble" lytic lesion; osteoclast-like giant cells driven by RANKL from stromal cells; locally aggressive; denosumab'],
['Osteoid osteoma','young adults, cortex of long bones','night pain relieved by NSAIDs (prostaglandins); lucent nidus under 2 cm with sclerosis'],
['Osteosarcoma','teenagers, metaphysis around the knee; elderly after Paget disease or radiation','Codman triangle, sunburst periosteal reaction; hereditary retinoblastoma (RB1), Li-Fraumeni (TP53)'],
['Ewing sarcoma','boys under 15, diaphysis of long bones and pelvis','onion-skin periosteal reaction; small round blue cells, CD99; t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1; responds to chemotherapy'],
['Chondrosarcoma','older adults, pelvis and central skeleton','malignant cartilage, expansile with ring-like calcification; poor response to chemotherapy']]},
s:[['pharm','Bone metastases: bisphosphonates or denosumab reduce fractures and hypercalcaemia; radiotherapy for pain; for prostate cancer, androgen deprivation. The paediatric hip and growth-plate disorders, and the nerves and joints of the limbs, are read in the Frame.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A 70-year-old man has bone pain, an enlarging hat size, deafness and an ALP of 900 U/L with normal calcium. Diagnosis?',o:['Osteomalacia','Primary hyperparathyroidism','Prostate metastases','Paget disease'],a:3,d:'path',c:'bone-disease',x:'Chaotic, overactive remodelling makes thick but weak bone that enlarges the skull and compresses the hearing apparatus, with a very high ALP and normal calcium and phosphate. Osteoblastic prostate metastases can also raise ALP, but they do not enlarge the skull and come with a raised PSA.'},
{q:'Which osteoporosis drug is an antibody against RANKL?',o:['Alendronate','Teriparatide','Denosumab','Raloxifene'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'osteoporosis',x:'Denosumab binds RANKL, doing the job of osteoprotegerin, so osteoclasts cannot form; stopping it abruptly causes rebound fractures. Alendronate also stops resorption, but by killing osteoclasts after binding hydroxyapatite.'},
{q:'A 12-year-old boy has a diaphyseal femoral lesion with an onion-skin periosteal reaction. Translocation?',o:['t(X;18)','t(14;18)','t(9;22)','t(11;22)'],a:3,d:'path',c:'bone-disease',x:'Ewing sarcoma carries t(11;22), fusing EWSR1 to FLI1; it is a CD99-positive small round blue cell tumour of the diaphysis that responds to chemotherapy. t(X;18) is synovial sarcoma, t(14;18) follicular lymphoma and t(9;22) the Philadelphia chromosome.'},
{q:'A man with sickle cell disease develops osteomyelitis. Which organism is characteristic and must be covered?',o:['Pseudomonas','Pasteurella','Salmonella','Mycobacterium'],a:2,d:'micro',c:'sickle-cell',x:'Salmonella is the characteristic organism in sickle cell osteomyelitis, although S. aureus is still common, so empirical cover must include both. Infarcted bone and a failed spleen let encapsulated gut organisms seed it; Pseudomonas follows a nail through a shoe.'},
{q:'A 17-year-old has night pain in the tibia relieved by aspirin, with a small lucent nidus on imaging. Diagnosis?',o:['Osteoid osteoma','Osteosarcoma','Giant cell tumour','Ewing sarcoma'],a:0,d:'path',c:'bone-disease',x:'A tiny osteoblastic nidus makes prostaglandins, so the pain is worst at night and melts with aspirin or NSAIDs. Osteosarcoma also strikes teenagers near the knee, but it is a destructive metaphyseal mass with a sunburst periosteal reaction.'},
{q:'Alendronate must be swallowed upright with a full glass of water. Which adverse effect does this prevent?',o:['Hypocalcaemia','Oesophageal injury','Osteonecrosis of the jaw','Renal toxicity'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'osteoporosis',x:'A tablet that lodges in the oesophagus causes erosive oesophagitis, so patients stay upright for 30 minutes; taking it fasting also matters, because food and calcium block its already tiny absorption. Osteonecrosis of the jaw is a rarer, dose-related risk that posture does not change.'},
{q:'A housebound woman has bone pain, proximal weakness and pseudofractures. Expected results?',o:['Ca ↑, PO₄ ↓, PTH ↑','Ca ↓, PO₄ ↓, PTH ↑','All normal','Ca normal, isolated ALP ↑↑'],a:1,d:'phys',c:'vitamins',x:'Vitamin D deficiency lowers gut absorption of calcium and phosphate; PTH rises to defend calcium and dumps phosphate into the urine, and ALP rises as osteoblasts struggle. High calcium with low phosphate is primary hyperparathyroidism, and normal labs fit osteoporosis.'}
]};

BOOK['c14-joint']={depth:14,title:'The Hinges',
epi:'Every hinge in the wall grinds: the old ones from wear, the young ones because the guards have turned on them.',
c:['rheumatoid-arthritis','osteoarthritis','spondyloarthritis','connective-tissue','gout-purines','hypersensitivity','collagen'],
S:[
{h:'Wear or inflammation',p:[
'**Osteoarthritis** is a failure of the whole joint, led by cartilage. With age, obesity, joint injury and malalignment, chondrocytes lose control of their matrix; IL-1 and matrix metalloproteinases break down type II collagen and proteoglycans, the cartilage fissures and thins, and the bone beneath answers with sclerosis, cysts and osteophytes. Pain is worse with use and at the end of the day, with short morning stiffness (under 30 minutes) and crepitus. It favours weight-bearing joints (knees, hips, lumbar spine), the **DIP (Heberden nodes)** and **PIP (Bouchard nodes)** joints and the first carpometacarpal joint. X-ray: joint space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis and cysts. No systemic features; paracetamol, topical and oral NSAIDs, exercise, weight loss, joint replacement.',
'**Rheumatoid arthritis** is an autoimmune synovitis. In genetically susceptible people (HLA-DR4 shared epitope), smoking and other triggers lead to citrullinated proteins and anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies; Th1 and Th17 cells, macrophages (TNF, IL-6) and B cells drive a proliferating synovium (**pannus**) that erodes cartilage and bone. Women are affected about three times as often. **Morning stiffness over an hour**, symmetric small joints of hands and feet (**MCP, PIP, wrists; DIP spared**), ulnar deviation, swan-neck and boutonnière deformities, rheumatoid nodules (central fibrinoid necrosis), Baker cyst, atlantoaxial subluxation (care at intubation), interstitial lung disease, pleural effusions with low glucose, scleritis, anaemia of chronic disease, Felty syndrome (RA, splenomegaly, neutropenia), AA amyloidosis, raised cardiovascular risk. Anti-CCP (specific) and rheumatoid factor (IgM against the Fc of IgG); erosions near the joint margins on X-ray.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Mechanism','Notes'],rows:[
['Methotrexate','DHFR inhibitor (low-dose weekly); first-line DMARD','give folic acid; mouth ulcers, liver fibrosis, pneumonitis, marrow suppression; teratogen'],
['Sulfasalazine','5-ASA plus sulfapyridine','reversible oligospermia, haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, rash'],
['Hydroxychloroquine','antimalarial; dampens antigen presentation and TLR signalling','retinopathy (yearly eye checks after 5 years)'],
['Leflunomide','inhibits pyrimidine synthesis (dihydroorotate dehydrogenase)','teratogen, liver toxicity'],
['Anti-TNF (adalimumab, etanercept, infliximab)','neutralise TNF (etanercept is a decoy receptor)','screen for latent TB and hepatitis B first; infections'],
['Tocilizumab, abatacept, rituximab','IL-6 receptor block; CTLA-4-Ig blocks CD80/86 co-stimulation; anti-CD20','infections; rituximab can reactivate hepatitis B'],
['JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, baricitinib)','block JAK-STAT cytokine signalling','infections, zoster, thrombosis, cardiovascular events']]},
key:'Osteoarthritis: worse with use, short stiffness, DIP and PIP, no systemic signs. Rheumatoid: worse after rest, long stiffness, MCP and PIP, DIP spared, systemic features.',
s:[['pharm','Glucocorticoids bridge the weeks before DMARDs work; NSAIDs ease pain but do not stop erosions. Start a DMARD early: most joint damage happens in the first two years.']]},
{h:'Crystals and infection',p:[
'**Gout**: monosodium urate crystals (**needle-shaped, negatively birefringent**: yellow when parallel to the compensator axis) precipitate in cool, peripheral joints when urate is high. Overproduction (Lesch-Nyhan syndrome, tumour lysis, myeloproliferative disease, psoriasis) or, far more often, underexcretion (kidney disease, thiazide and loop diuretics, alcohol, low-dose aspirin, ciclosporin, pyrazinamide). Attacks follow alcohol, purine-rich meals, dehydration, surgery or a sudden change in urate. First MTP joint (podagra), tophi (chalky deposits with giant cells), urate kidney stones and urate nephropathy. Acute: NSAIDs, colchicine or steroids. Long term: **allopurinol or febuxostat** (xanthine oxidase inhibitors), probenecid (uricosuric), pegloticase. Do not stop urate-lowering therapy during a flare; it can be started during one if colchicine or NSAID prophylaxis is given, and that cover continues for 3–6 months.',
'**Pseudogout** (calcium pyrophosphate deposition): **rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent** crystals, often in the knee and wrist of older people, with chondrocalcinosis on X-ray. In younger patients look for haemochromatosis, hyperparathyroidism, hypomagnesaemia or hypophosphatasia.',
'**Septic arthritis**: S. aureus most often; N. gonorrhoeae in sexually active young adults (migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis and a few pustules, or a single hot knee); S. epidermidis in prosthetic joints; Salmonella in sickle cell disease. Aspirate before antibiotics; then drain the joint and give IV antibiotics, because cartilage is destroyed within days. **Lyme arthritis** (often a large-joint, recurrent knee) and viral arthritis (parvovirus B19, hepatitis B, rubella) mimic early RA.'],
table:{cols:['Synovial fluid','Appearance','White cells /µL','Clue'],rows:[
['Normal','clear, viscous','under 200','—'],
['Non-inflammatory (OA, trauma)','clear yellow','200–2,000','under 25% neutrophils'],
['Inflammatory (RA, crystals, spondyloarthritis)','cloudy, less viscous','2,000–50,000','crystals under polarised light in gout and pseudogout'],
['Septic','turbid, purulent','over 50,000','over 75% neutrophils, low glucose; Gram stain and culture'],
['Haemorrhagic','bloody','variable','trauma, haemophilia, anticoagulants']]},
s:[['pharm','Allopurinol: start low, especially in kidney disease; Stevens-Johnson syndrome and DRESS (HLA-B*58:01, test first in high-risk ancestry); it blocks the breakdown of azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine, which then reach toxic levels. Febuxostat carries a cardiovascular death warning. Probenecid fails in kidney disease and should not be used in overproducers or stone formers. Pegloticase (recombinant uricase) is contraindicated in G6PD deficiency; rasburicase prevents tumour lysis. Colchicine binds tubulin and blocks neutrophil migration; diarrhoea, and toxicity with CYP3A4 inhibitors such as clarithromycin.']]},
{h:'Spondyloarthritis and connective tissue disease',p:[
'**Seronegative spondyloarthritides** (HLA-B27, rheumatoid factor negative) inflame the **entheses**, where tendons and ligaments insert into bone. **Ankylosing spondylitis**: young men, inflammatory back pain that improves with exercise and is worse at night and after rest, sacroiliitis, syndesmophytes fusing into a "bamboo spine", restricted chest expansion, anterior uveitis, aortitis with aortic regurgitation, heel enthesitis. **Psoriatic arthritis**: DIP disease, dactylitis ("sausage digits"), nail pitting and onycholysis, pencil-in-cup erosions, arthritis mutilans. **Reactive arthritis**: 1–4 weeks after Chlamydia or gut infection (Campylobacter, Shigella, Salmonella, Yersinia): arthritis, urethritis, conjunctivitis, keratoderma blennorrhagicum. **IBD-associated arthritis** tracks the bowel (peripheral) or runs its own course (axial). Treat with NSAIDs and exercise; anti-TNF or anti-IL-17 (secukinumab) for axial disease.',
'**Systemic sclerosis**: endothelial injury and fibroblast activation lay down excess collagen in skin and organs. **Limited (CREST)**: calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, oesophageal dysmotility (reflux, strictures), sclerodactyly, telangiectasia; anti-centromere; late pulmonary arterial hypertension. **Diffuse**: anti-Scl-70 (topoisomerase I); trunk skin, early lung fibrosis, and **scleroderma renal crisis** (anti-RNA polymerase III; avoid high-dose steroids, which can trigger it; treat with ACE inhibitors). **Sjögren syndrome**: lymphocytes destroy salivary and lacrimal glands: dry eyes and mouth, dental caries, parotid swelling, anti-Ro and anti-La, a raised risk of MALT lymphoma; anti-Ro in pregnancy can cause congenital heart block. **Polymyositis** (CD8 T cells, endomysial) and **dermatomyositis** (CD4 T cells and complement, perimysial and perifascicular atrophy; heliotrope rash, Gottron papules, shawl sign; often paraneoplastic): symmetric proximal weakness, raised CK, anti-Jo-1 with interstitial lung disease. **Polymyalgia rheumatica**: over 50, painful stiffness of the shoulder and hip girdles, high ESR, normal CK, linked with giant cell arteritis; responds quickly to low-dose steroids. Lupus and antiphospholipid syndrome are read in the Overreaction.'],
table:{cols:['Disease','Antibody','Hallmark'],rows:[
['Rheumatoid arthritis','anti-CCP (specific), rheumatoid factor','erosive symmetric small-joint arthritis'],
['Limited systemic sclerosis','anti-centromere','skin of hands and face; late pulmonary hypertension'],
['Diffuse systemic sclerosis','anti-Scl-70; anti-RNA polymerase III','trunk skin, early lung fibrosis; RNA polymerase III: renal crisis'],
['Sjögren syndrome','anti-Ro (SSA), anti-La (SSB)','dry eyes and mouth; MALT lymphoma; neonatal heart block'],
['Polymyositis, dermatomyositis','anti-Jo-1 (lung fibrosis), anti-Mi-2 (dermatomyositis)','proximal weakness, raised CK'],
['Mixed connective tissue disease','anti-U1 RNP','overlap of lupus, scleroderma and myositis; Raynaud']]},
s:[['path','Fibromyalgia: widespread chronic pain from altered central pain processing, with fatigue, poor sleep and tender points, but normal inflammatory markers and CK; exercise, sleep care, SNRIs (duloxetine), pregabalin.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'Synovial fluid shows needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals. Which long-term drug inhibits xanthine oxidase?',o:['Colchicine','Probenecid','Allopurinol','Naproxen'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'gout-purines',x:'Allopurinol (like febuxostat) inhibits xanthine oxidase and lowers urate production for long-term prevention. Colchicine and NSAIDs such as naproxen treat the acute flare but do not lower urate, and probenecid lowers it by increasing renal excretion.'},
{q:'Symmetric MCP and PIP swelling with 2 hours of morning stiffness. Which joint is typically spared?',o:['Wrist','MCP','PIP','DIP'],a:3,d:'path',c:'rheumatoid-arthritis',x:'Rheumatoid synovitis attacks the MCP, PIP and wrist joints symmetrically and characteristically spares the DIP joints. DIP involvement points instead to osteoarthritis (Heberden nodes) or psoriatic arthritis.'},
{q:'A young man has low back pain that improves with exercise and reduced chest expansion. Associated valve lesion?',o:['Aortic regurgitation','Mitral stenosis','Tricuspid stenosis','Pulmonary stenosis'],a:0,d:'path',c:'spondyloarthritis',x:'Ankylosing spondylitis inflames the aortic root, which dilates the valve ring and causes aortic regurgitation; anterior uveitis is the other classic extra-articular feature. Mitral stenosis follows rheumatic fever and has nothing to do with HLA-B27.'},
{q:'Diffuse scleroderma with sudden severe hypertension and rising creatinine. Treatment?',o:['Steroids','β-blocker','ACE inhibitor','Dialysis'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'connective-tissue',x:'Scleroderma renal crisis is driven by renin from narrowed renal arteries, so an ACE inhibitor is life-saving and is continued even if creatinine rises. High-dose steroids can precipitate the crisis, which is why they are avoided in diffuse disease.'},
{q:'A 60-year-old has a heliotrope rash, Gottron papules and proximal weakness. What should be screened for?',o:['Chronic hepatitis C','HIV infection','Autoimmune thyroid disease','Underlying malignancy'],a:3,d:'path',c:'connective-tissue',x:'Dermatomyositis in an older adult is often paraneoplastic (ovary, lung, gut, breast), so screen for cancer at diagnosis and again in the following years. Hepatitis C is linked to cryoglobulinaemia and lichen planus, not to heliotrope rash.'},
{q:'A hot, swollen knee; the aspirate is turbid with 85,000 white cells/µL, mostly neutrophils, and no crystals. Next step?',o:['Drain and give IV antibiotics','Oral colchicine and rest','Steroid injection into the joint','Start allopurinol today'],a:0,d:'micro',c:'gram-positive-cocci',x:'Over 50,000 white cells/µL with neutrophils dominating means septic arthritis until proven otherwise; S. aureus is commonest, so drain the joint and start IV antibiotics before cartilage is destroyed. Injecting a steroid into an infected joint would be dangerous.'},
{q:'A 52-year-old has knee pseudogout with chondrocalcinosis and rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent crystals. Which condition should be screened for?',o:['Lesch-Nyhan syndrome','Haemochromatosis','Psoriasis','Sarcoidosis'],a:1,d:'path',c:'osteoarthritis',x:'Calcium pyrophosphate deposition at a younger age suggests haemochromatosis, hyperparathyroidism, hypomagnesaemia or hypophosphatasia. Lesch-Nyhan syndrome overproduces urate, which forms needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals.'}
]};

BOOK['c14-muscle']={depth:14,title:'The Cables',
epi:'Cables of muscle run the length of the wall and pull when the signal comes. Where they have frayed, or gone deaf to the signal, the wall sags.',
c:['muscle-disease','rhabdomyolysis','nmj','excitability'],
S:[
{h:'Contraction',fig:'sarc',p:[
'Skeletal muscle: acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction opens nicotinic channels, the action potential spreads down the **T-tubules**, the **dihydropyridine receptor** (an L-type Ca²⁺ channel acting as a voltage sensor) mechanically opens the **ryanodine receptor** (RyR1), Ca²⁺ leaves the sarcoplasmic reticulum, binds **troponin C**, and tropomyosin rolls away from the myosin-binding sites on actin. Cross-bridge cycle: ATP binding releases myosin from actin; ATP hydrolysis cocks the head; release of phosphate drives the power stroke. Relaxation needs ATP too, for SERCA to pump Ca²⁺ back into the reticulum. Without ATP, myosin stays bound (rigor mortis).',
'In the sarcomere the **A band** (the length of the myosin filaments) stays constant; the **H band** and **I band** shorten and the Z lines move closer. Force is greatest near the resting sarcomere length, where actin and myosin overlap best. **Type I** fibres are slow, oxidative and red (rich in myoglobin and mitochondria; posture, endurance); **type II** are fast, glycolytic and white (sprinting). The first seconds of effort run on stored ATP and creatine phosphate, then glycolysis, then oxidative phosphorylation. Smooth muscle has no troponin: Ca²⁺ binds **calmodulin**, which activates **myosin light-chain kinase**; nitric oxide relaxes it through cGMP, which activates myosin light-chain phosphatase.'],
table:{cols:['Feature','Skeletal','Cardiac','Smooth'],rows:[
['Trigger','motor nerve (ACh, nicotinic)','pacemaker spread through gap junctions','nerves, hormones, stretch'],
['Ca²⁺ source','sarcoplasmic reticulum only (mechanical DHPR–RyR1 link)','Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release: L-type entry opens RyR2','extracellular and SR (IP₃ receptors)'],
['Ca²⁺ sensor','troponin C','troponin C','calmodulin → myosin light-chain kinase'],
['Striations','yes','yes','no'],
['Tetanic summation','yes','no (long refractory period)','sustained tone (latch state)']]},
s:[['pharm','Malignant hyperthermia: RYR1 mutation (autosomal dominant); volatile anaesthetics or succinylcholine trigger uncontrolled Ca²⁺ release: masseter and generalised rigidity, rising end-tidal CO₂, tachycardia, hyperthermia, hyperkalaemia, rhabdomyolysis. Stop the trigger and give dantrolene, which blocks the ryanodine receptor. Neuromuscular blockers: succinylcholine (depolarising; hyperkalaemia in burns, crush injury and denervation; prolonged apnoea with pseudocholinesterase deficiency) and rocuronium or vecuronium (non-depolarising; reversed by neostigmine with glycopyrrolate, or by sugammadex, which encapsulates them).']]},
{h:'The junction and its diseases',p:[
'**Myasthenia gravis**: IgG against the postsynaptic **acetylcholine receptor** (or MuSK) blocks and destroys receptors through complement (type II hypersensitivity), so each impulse produces a smaller end-plate potential. Fatigable weakness worse at the end of the day: ptosis and diplopia first, then bulbar and limb weakness; normal reflexes and sensation. Thymic hyperplasia or thymoma (image the mediastinum). Diagnose with antibodies and a decremental response to repetitive nerve stimulation. Pyridostigmine (acetylcholinesterase inhibitor), steroids and other immunosuppressants, complement or FcRn inhibitors, thymectomy; myasthenic crisis (respiratory failure) needs ventilation, IVIG or plasma exchange. Drugs that worsen it: aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, macrolides, magnesium, β-blockers. Too much pyridostigmine causes a cholinergic crisis, which also brings weakness.',
'**Lambert-Eaton syndrome**: antibodies to presynaptic **voltage-gated calcium channels**, often paraneoplastic (small cell lung cancer): proximal leg weakness that **improves briefly with repeated effort**, reduced reflexes, dry mouth and other autonomic symptoms; an incremental response to fast repetitive stimulation. Treat the tumour; amifampridine blocks presynaptic K⁺ channels, prolonging depolarisation so more Ca²⁺ enters. **Botulism**: the toxin cleaves SNARE proteins and stops acetylcholine release: descending symmetrical flaccid paralysis with fixed dilated pupils, cranial nerves first; infants acquire it from spores (honey, soil), adults from preformed toxin in poorly canned food or from wounds; antitoxin.'],
table:{cols:['Finding','Myasthenia gravis','Lambert-Eaton'],rows:[
['Antibody target','postsynaptic ACh receptor','presynaptic Ca²⁺ channel'],
['Repeated use','worsens','improves briefly'],
['Reflexes','normal','reduced'],
['Autonomic symptoms','none','dry mouth, constipation, impotence'],
['Association','thymoma, thymic hyperplasia','small cell lung carcinoma'],
['Eye muscles','commonly first','less often'],
['Treatment','pyridostigmine, immunosuppression, thymectomy','treat the tumour; amifampridine']]},
s:[['micro','Clostridium botulinum toxin blocks acetylcholine release at the junction (flaccid paralysis); Clostridium tetani toxin travels back up the motor nerve and blocks glycine and GABA release from inhibitory interneurons in the cord (spastic paralysis, trismus, risus sardonicus, opisthotonus). Both are zinc proteases that cleave SNARE proteins.']]},
{h:'The dystrophies and myopathies',p:[
'**Dystrophin** anchors the actin skeleton of each muscle fibre, through the membrane, to the extracellular matrix. Without it every contraction tears the sarcolemma, Ca²⁺ floods in, fibres die and are replaced by fat and fibrous tissue. A **frameshift** deletion destroys the reading frame and the protein (Duchenne); an **in-frame** deletion leaves a shorter, partly working protein (Becker). The DMD gene is one of the largest in the genome, so new mutations are common (about one third of cases).',
'**Myotonic dystrophy type 1**: autosomal dominant CTG repeat in the 3′ untranslated region of DMPK, with anticipation (a severe congenital form passes through mothers). Myotonia (cannot release a grip), distal and facial weakness, ptosis, frontal balding, early cataracts, testicular atrophy, insulin resistance and cardiac conduction block. **Inflammatory myopathies** are read in the Hinges.'],
table:{cols:['Disorder','Defect','Clues'],rows:[
['Duchenne muscular dystrophy','X-linked frameshift deletion in dystrophin','boys by 5; Gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy (fat, fibrosis), very high CK, dilated cardiomyopathy, scoliosis, respiratory failure'],
['Becker muscular dystrophy','X-linked in-frame deletion: some dystrophin','later onset and milder; walking into adulthood; cardiomyopathy'],
['Myotonic dystrophy type 1','CTG repeat in DMPK (autosomal dominant)','myotonia, cataracts, balding, testicular atrophy, conduction block'],
['McArdle disease','muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency (glycogen storage type V)','exercise cramps and myoglobinuria; a "second wind"; no lactate rise on exercise testing'],
['Mitochondrial myopathy','mitochondrial DNA mutations; maternal inheritance, heteroplasmy','ragged red fibres; MELAS, MERRF'],
['Inclusion body myositis','degenerative and inflammatory','over 50; finger flexor and quadriceps weakness; steroid-resistant']]},
s:[['bio','Gowers sign is the child pushing up on his own thighs to stand, because the hip extensors are weak. Female carriers of Duchenne can develop cardiomyopathy. Exon-skipping antisense drugs restore the reading frame in some Duchenne deletions, turning Duchenne towards a Becker-like protein.']]},
{h:'Rhabdomyolysis and statins',p:[
'Muscle breakdown releases **CK**, myoglobin, potassium, phosphate and urate. Causes: crush injury and compartment syndrome, prolonged immobilisation (a long lie after a fall), seizures, extreme exercise, heat stroke, malignant hyperthermia, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, cocaine and amphetamines, alcohol, hypokalaemia, hypothyroidism, McArdle disease and statins. Urine is dark and dipstick-positive for blood without red cells on microscopy; myoglobin precipitates in acidic tubules and its haem injures them (acute tubular necrosis). Early hypocalcaemia (calcium binds to damaged muscle), sometimes rebound hypercalcaemia in recovery; hyperkalaemia can stop the heart. Large volumes of IV fluid; treat hyperkalaemia; calcium only for dangerous hyperkalaemia or tetany.',
'**Statin myopathy** ranges from aches with a normal CK to rhabdomyolysis. Risk rises with high doses, older age, hypothyroidism, kidney impairment, the SLCO1B1 variant, drugs that block statin metabolism (CYP3A4 inhibitors with simvastatin or atorvastatin: macrolides, azole antifungals, HIV protease inhibitors, large amounts of grapefruit juice) and gemfibrozil. Check CK when symptoms appear; stop the statin if CK exceeds 10 times the upper limit or symptoms are severe.'],
s:[['bio','Creatine kinase isoforms: MM in skeletal muscle, MB in heart, BB in brain. After a myocardial infarction CK-MB rises within 3–8 hours, peaks at about 24 hours and returns to normal within 2–3 days, so a second rise marks reinfarction; troponin stays raised for 7–10 days and has replaced CK-MB for diagnosis.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'Which band of the sarcomere stays the same length during contraction?',o:['I band','H band','Sarcomere length','A band'],a:3,d:'phys',c:'muscle-disease',x:'The A band equals the length of the myosin filaments, which do not shorten; the filaments slide past one another. The I and H bands, the zones without overlap, shrink as the Z lines approach.'},
{q:'Muscle rigidity, rising end-tidal CO₂ and fever during a sevoflurane anaesthetic. Treatment?',o:['Paracetamol','Bromocriptine','Dantrolene','Cyproheptadine'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'muscle-disease',x:'Volatile anaesthetics open a mutant RYR1 and flood the cytosol with calcium, causing rigidity, hypercarbia and fever; dantrolene blocks the ryanodine receptor. Bromocriptine is for neuroleptic malignant syndrome and cyproheptadine for serotonin syndrome.'},
{q:'A smoker has proximal weakness that improves briefly after repeated contraction, with reduced reflexes. Antibody target?',o:['Postsynaptic ACh receptor','Muscle-specific kinase (MuSK)','Ryanodine receptor','Voltage-gated calcium channel'],a:3,d:'path',c:'nmj',x:'In Lambert-Eaton syndrome antibodies block presynaptic calcium channels, and repeated firing lets calcium build up so release improves briefly; it is often paraneoplastic with small cell lung cancer. Antibodies against the ACh receptor cause myasthenia gravis, which worsens with use and spares reflexes.'},
{q:'A 5-year-old boy climbs up his own legs to stand and has enlarged calves. Type of gene defect?',o:['Missense point mutation in dystrophin','Frameshift deletion in dystrophin','CTG repeat expansion','CAG repeat expansion'],a:1,d:'bio',c:'muscle-disease',x:'Duchenne deletions shift the reading frame, so no working dystrophin is made; Gowers sign and calf pseudohypertrophy follow. In-frame deletions leave a shortened protein and give the milder Becker dystrophy, and CTG repeats cause myotonic dystrophy.'},
{q:'Why is succinylcholine dangerous in a patient with extensive burns?',o:['Hyperkalaemia','Hypothermia','Bradycardia','Anaphylaxis'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'nmj',x:'After burns, crush injury or denervation, immature acetylcholine receptors spread over the whole muscle membrane, and succinylcholine depolarises them all at once, releasing enough potassium to stop the heart. A non-depolarising blocker such as rocuronium avoids this.'},
{q:'A woman with myasthenia gravis becomes weaker and breathless after starting a drug for pneumonia. Most likely culprit?',o:['Amoxicillin','Gentamicin','Ceftriaxone','Doxycycline'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'nmj',x:'Aminoglycosides reduce acetylcholine release and stabilise the postsynaptic membrane, and can tip myasthenia into crisis; fluoroquinolones, macrolides, magnesium and β-blockers do the same. β-lactams such as amoxicillin and ceftriaxone are generally safe.'},
{q:'After a long seizure, the urine dipstick is strongly positive for blood, but microscopy shows no red cells. What is in the urine?',o:['Haemoglobin','Bilirubin','Porphyrins','Myoglobin'],a:3,d:'path',c:'rhabdomyolysis',x:'The dipstick detects haem in both haemoglobin and myoglobin; with no red cells after a seizure and a very high CK, myoglobin from rhabdomyolysis is the cause. Haemolysis also gives haem without red cells, but the plasma is pink and CK is normal.'}
]};

BOOK['c14-skin']={depth:14,title:'The Rampart',
epi:'The outermost wall. The Bassiet came in through it, at the back of the left hand.',
c:['blistering','skin-cancer','hypersensitivity','p53-rb','collagen','wound-healing'],
S:[
{h:'Layers and lesions',p:[
'Epidermis from bottom to top: **stratum basale** (dividing stem cells, melanocytes), **spinosum** (keratinocytes joined by desmosomes; Langerhans cells), **granulosum** (keratohyalin granules, lipid barrier), **lucidum** (thick skin of palms and soles only), **corneum** (dead keratin). Keratinocytes hold each other with **desmosomes** (desmogleins 1 and 3) and hold the basement membrane with **hemidesmosomes** (BP180, BP230, integrins). Melanocytes come from the neural crest and pass melanin to keratinocytes; Langerhans cells (Birbeck granules) carry antigen to the nodes. The dermis holds collagen, vessels, nerves, glands and hair follicles.',
'The words of the wall are the language of every skin vignette. A lesion is described by type, colour, shape, distribution and what lies on its surface; histology adds its own vocabulary for what has happened in each layer.'],
table:{cols:['Word','Meaning'],rows:[
['Macule / patch','flat change of colour, under / over 1 cm'],
['Papule / plaque','raised solid lesion, under / over 1 cm'],
['Vesicle / bulla','fluid-filled blister, under / over 1 cm'],
['Pustule','blister filled with pus'],
['Wheal','transient dermal oedema (urticaria)'],
['Scale, crust','flakes of corneum; dried exudate'],
['Lichenification','thick, leathery skin from scratching'],
['Hyperkeratosis','thickened stratum corneum'],
['Parakeratosis','nuclei retained in the stratum corneum (psoriasis)'],
['Acanthosis','thickened stratum spinosum'],
['Acantholysis','keratinocytes separating from each other (pemphigus)'],
['Spongiosis','oedema between keratinocytes (eczema)']]}},
{h:'Inflammation of the wall',p:[
'Most rashes are the immune system at work in the epidermis. Eczema is spongiosis; psoriasis is a T-cell–driven race of keratinocytes (a cell reaches the surface in days instead of a month); lichen planus is cytotoxic T cells biting the basal layer; urticaria is mast cells emptying in the dermis. Several common lesions are better read as signals from deeper organs.'],
table:{cols:['Condition','Mechanism and clues'],rows:[
['Psoriasis','Th17/IL-23 axis; well-demarcated silvery plaques on extensor surfaces and scalp, Auspitz sign, Koebner phenomenon, nail pitting; parakeratosis, Munro microabscesses, acanthosis; arthritis'],
['Atopic dermatitis','filaggrin barrier defects, Th2; itchy eczema of face and extensors in infants, flexures later; asthma and hay fever'],
['Contact dermatitis','allergic: type IV (nickel, poison ivy, fragrances, neomycin) at the site of contact; irritant: direct injury'],
['Urticaria','type I mast cell degranulation (or physical, or idiopathic); itchy wheals that clear within 24 hours; antihistamines'],
['Lichen planus','pruritic purple polygonal flat papules, Wickham striae, oral white lace; saw-tooth rete ridges, band of T cells; hepatitis C'],
['Seborrhoeic dermatitis','greasy yellow scale on scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds; Malassezia; cradle cap; severe in HIV and Parkinson disease'],
['Acne vulgaris','androgen-driven sebum, follicular plugging (comedones), Cutibacterium acnes, inflammation; retinoids, benzoyl peroxide, antibiotics, isotretinoin'],
['Rosacea','central facial flushing, papules and pustules, telangiectasia, rhinophyma; no comedones'],
['Pityriasis rosea','herald patch, then oval scaly plaques along skin lines ("Christmas tree"); possibly HHV-6/7; fades in 6–8 weeks'],
['Erythema nodosum','tender red nodules on the shins; septal panniculitis; sarcoidosis, IBD, streptococcal infection, TB, coccidioidomycosis, pregnancy, the pill'],
['Acanthosis nigricans','velvety dark skin of neck and axillae; insulin resistance; sudden onset with gastric adenocarcinoma'],
['Seborrhoeic keratosis','"stuck-on" waxy brown lesions, horn cysts; Leser-Trélat sign (sudden eruption) with GI cancer'],
['Verruca (wart)','HPV; koilocytes; hands and feet']]},
s:[['pharm','Psoriasis: topical steroids and vitamin D analogues (calcipotriol), phototherapy, methotrexate, ciclosporin, apremilast (PDE-4 inhibitor), biologics against TNF, IL-17 (secukinumab), IL-23 (guselkumab) or IL-12/23 (ustekinumab). Long use of potent topical steroids thins the skin. Isotretinoin for severe nodular acne: highly teratogenic (two forms of contraception and monthly pregnancy tests), dry skin and lips, raised lipids and liver enzymes, and mood change.']]},
{h:'Blisters and the cleavage plane',p:[
'Where the skin splits decides what a blister looks like. A split high in the epidermis leaves a thin roof that tears easily: flaccid blisters and raw erosions that spread under a sliding finger (Nikolsky sign). A split beneath the epidermis leaves a full-thickness roof: tense blisters that hold. Antibodies against desmosomes split keratinocytes apart; antibodies against hemidesmosomes lift the whole epidermis off the basement membrane.'],
table:{cols:['Disease','Target and split','Immunofluorescence','Clues'],rows:[
['Pemphigus vulgaris','IgG against desmoglein 3 (and 1); within the epidermis, just above the basal layer (acantholysis, "tombstone" basal cells)','intercellular IgG, net-like ("fishnet")','flaccid bullae, mouth first, Nikolsky +; type II hypersensitivity; steroids, rituximab'],
['Pemphigus foliaceus','IgG against desmoglein 1; high, under the stratum corneum','intercellular IgG, superficial','crusted erosions, mouth spared'],
['Bullous pemphigoid','IgG against hemidesmosomes (BP180, BP230); below the epidermis','linear IgG and C3 along the basement membrane','tense itchy bullae, mouth usually spared, older people, Nikolsky −'],
['Dermatitis herpetiformis','IgA against epidermal transglutaminase; below the epidermis at the papillae','granular IgA in dermal papillae','intensely itchy grouped vesicles on elbows, knees, buttocks; coeliac disease; dapsone and gluten-free diet'],
['Staphylococcal scalded skin','exfoliative toxin cleaves desmoglein 1; stratum granulosum','none','young children; tender red skin peels in sheets, mucosa spared, heals without scars'],
['Stevens-Johnson / toxic epidermal necrolysis','cytotoxic T cells kill keratinocytes; full-thickness necrosis, split at the dermal-epidermal junction','none','fever, mucosal erosions, under 10% (SJS) or over 30% (TEN) of skin; drugs'],
['Erythema multiforme','cell-mediated reaction to infection','none','target lesions on hands and feet; HSV most often, Mycoplasma']]},
s:[['pharm','Drugs that cause SJS and TEN: sulfonamides (including co-trimoxazole), allopurinol (HLA-B*58:01), carbamazepine (HLA-B*15:02 in people of Southeast Asian ancestry), lamotrigine (rapid dose increases), phenytoin, phenobarbital, nevirapine, NSAIDs of the oxicam group. Stop the drug at once and manage like a burn.']]},
{h:'Pigment and skin cancer',p:[
'**Vitiligo**: autoimmune loss of melanocytes; sharply defined white patches. **Albinism**: normal melanocyte numbers but no melanin (tyrosinase defect in oculocutaneous type 1); skin cancer risk. **Melasma**: symmetric facial hyperpigmentation in pregnancy or on the pill. **Freckles** have more pigment, not more melanocytes; a **lentigo** has more melanocytes.',
'**Actinic keratosis**: rough scaly papules on sun-damaged skin; keratinocyte dysplasia; a precursor of squamous cell carcinoma. **Basal cell carcinoma** (the commonest skin cancer): sun-exposed upper face, a pearly papule with rolled edges and telangiectasia, sometimes ulcerated ("rodent ulcer"); palisading nuclei at the edge of nests; locally destructive but rarely metastasises; Hedgehog pathway (PTCH1). **Squamous cell carcinoma**: sun, arsenic, immunosuppression (much commoner after transplant), HPV, chronic wounds and burn scars (Marjolin ulcer); lower lip, ears, backs of hands; keratin pearls; can metastasise. **Keratoacanthoma**: a dome with a keratin-filled crater that grows fast and regresses. **Melanoma**: ABCDE (asymmetry, irregular border, uneven colour, diameter over 6 mm, evolving); **Breslow thickness** (depth) is the strongest prognostic factor; S-100, HMB-45, Melan-A; BRAF V600E in about half; dysplastic naevus syndrome (CDKN2A). Subtypes: superficial spreading (commonest), nodular (vertical growth from the start), lentigo maligna (sun-damaged faces of the elderly), acral lentiginous (palms, soles, nail beds; commonest in darker skin; not sun-related). **Kaposi sarcoma**: HHV-8; purple plaques and nodules of spindle cells and slit-like vessels; AIDS, transplant recipients, older Mediterranean men.'],
key:'Pearly with rolled edges: basal cell, local damage only. Scaly on a lower lip or old scar: squamous cell, can spread. Deep and dark: melanoma, and depth is the prognosis.',
s:[['micro','Skin infections: impetigo (S. aureus or S. pyogenes; honey-coloured crusts; bullous impetigo is staphylococcal), erysipelas (S. pyogenes; raised, sharply demarcated, upper dermis and lymphatics), cellulitis (S. pyogenes and S. aureus; deeper dermis and fat, ill-defined), necrotising fasciitis (S. pyogenes or mixed anaerobes; pain out of proportion, crepitus, rapid spread along fascia; urgent surgery), staphylococcal scalded skin (exfoliative toxin cleaves desmoglein 1 in the stratum granulosum; toxic epidermal necrolysis splits deeper, at the dermal-epidermal junction), molluscum contagiosum (poxvirus; umbilicated papules), tinea (dermatophytes; ring with central clearing).'],['pharm','Melanoma: BRAF inhibitors (vemurafenib, dabrafenib) with MEK inhibitors for BRAF V600E tumours; checkpoint inhibitors against PD-1 (pembrolizumab, nivolumab) and CTLA-4 (ipilimumab), which can cause autoimmune colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis and hypophysitis. Advanced basal cell carcinoma: vismodegib (Smoothened inhibitor).']]},
{h:'Wounds and the left hand',p:[
'Skin protects against water loss, infection and ultraviolet light, regulates temperature through sweat and blood flow, makes vitamin D and senses. **Burns**: superficial (epidermis only; red and painful, no blisters), partial thickness (blisters, painful, pink; deep partial thickness heals slowly with scarring), full thickness (white or leathery, painless because nerve endings are destroyed; needs grafting). Burn patients lose plasma through leaky capillaries: the Parkland formula gives 4 mL × kg × % body surface area of Ringer lactate over 24 hours, half in the first 8 hours from the time of the burn; ATLS now starts adults at 2 mL × kg × % surface area and titrates to a urine output of 0.5 mL/kg/h. Circumferential full-thickness burns may need escharotomy; soot, singed nasal hair and hoarseness warn of inhalation injury (and carbon monoxide or cyanide). Later come Pseudomonas wound infection, stress (Curling) ulcers and a long hypermetabolic state.',
'A needlestick from an HIV-positive source carries about a 0.3% risk of transmission; hepatitis B 6–30% in the unvaccinated (highest when the source is HBeAg positive); hepatitis C about 0.2–2%. Wash the wound, report it, and assess and test the source. HIV: start post-exposure prophylaxis as soon as possible, ideally within 2 hours and no later than 72, and continue for 28 days. HBV: if unvaccinated or a known non-responder, give hepatitis B immunoglobulin and start vaccination; a vaccinated responder needs nothing. HCV: there is no prophylaxis; test for HCV RNA at 3–6 weeks and treat early infection. Report it, however small it seems: an exposure that is never reported can never be followed up.'],
s:[['phys','Wound healing: haemostasis, then inflammation (neutrophils, then macrophages), proliferation (granulation tissue from about day 3, type III collagen, contraction by myofibroblasts) and remodelling (type I collagen, strength reaching about 80% of normal by 3 months). Re-epithelialisation comes from basal stem cells and the hair follicle bulge. Keloids and hypertrophic scars are read in the Scar Fields.'],['micro','Burn and wound infections: Pseudomonas aeruginosa (blue-green pus, grape-like odour), S. aureus, S. pyogenes. Tetanus-prone wounds need a booster, and immunoglobulin if immunisation is incomplete.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'Silvery plaques on the elbows with nail pitting. Key cytokine axis?',o:['IL-4/IL-13','IFN-α/IFN-β','IL-17/IL-23','IL-5/eotaxin'],a:2,d:'path',c:'blistering',x:'Psoriasis is driven by IL-23 from dendritic cells, which sustains Th17 cells whose IL-17 makes keratinocytes proliferate; biologics against IL-17 and IL-23 work for this reason. IL-4 and IL-13 drive the Th2 inflammation of atopic dermatitis.'},
{q:'A pearly papule with rolled edges and telangiectasia on the nose. Tumour and metastatic risk?',o:['Melanoma; high','Squamous cell carcinoma; moderate','Seborrhoeic keratosis; none','Basal cell carcinoma; very low'],a:3,d:'path',c:'skin-cancer',x:'Basal cell carcinoma is the commonest skin cancer and destroys tissue locally but almost never metastasises. Squamous cell carcinoma on a scaly, sun-damaged site or scar is the one that can spread.'},
{q:'Single best prognostic factor in melanoma?',o:['Breslow thickness','Lesion diameter','Asymmetry','Patient age'],a:0,d:'path',c:'skin-cancer',x:'Breslow thickness, the depth of invasion in millimetres, predicts the chance of spread to nodes and beyond better than any surface feature. Diameter and asymmetry help to recognise a melanoma, but not to predict its course.'},
{q:'Intensely itchy grouped vesicles on the elbows and knees in a patient with chronic diarrhoea. Treatment?',o:['Dapsone, gluten-free diet','Oral aciclovir for 7 days','Potent topical steroid alone','Oral terbinafine course'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'blistering',x:'Dermatitis herpetiformis is the skin form of coeliac disease, with IgA deposits in the dermal papillae; dapsone controls the itch quickly and a gluten-free diet treats the cause. Grouped vesicles can suggest herpes, but herpes is not linked to diarrhoea or IgA.'},
{q:'After a needlestick from an HIV-positive source, when should post-exposure prophylaxis start?',o:['Only after the source test result','Within 2 hours','After 1 week','Only if symptoms appear'],a:1,d:'micro',c:'hiv',x:'Prophylaxis works best when started as soon as possible, ideally within 2 hours and no later than 72, and runs for 28 days; never wait for the source result. Waiting for symptoms means waiting for seroconversion, when prophylaxis can no longer help.'},
{q:'An 80-year-old has tense, itchy bullae on the trunk, and the mouth is spared. What does direct immunofluorescence show?',o:['Intercellular IgG in a fishnet pattern','Linear basement membrane IgG and C3','Granular IgA in the dermal papillae','No immune deposits'],a:1,d:'path',c:'blistering',x:'Bullous pemphigoid antibodies target hemidesmosomes (BP180, BP230), so IgG and C3 line the basement membrane and the blister is subepidermal and tense. The net-like intercellular pattern belongs to pemphigus, whose blisters lie within the epidermis, are flaccid and start in the mouth.'},
{q:'A 3-year-old has fever and tender red skin that peels in sheets with light pressure; the mouth is spared. Where does the skin split?',o:['Dermal-epidermal junction','Stratum granulosum','Just above the basal layer','Within the dermis'],a:1,d:'micro',c:'gram-positive-cocci',x:'Staphylococcal exfoliative toxins cleave desmoglein 1 high in the epidermis, in the stratum granulosum, so the split is superficial, the mucosa is spared and the skin heals without scars. Toxic epidermal necrolysis splits at the dermal-epidermal junction and involves the mucosa.'}
]};
